# Rule: SwiftData Relationships

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/defining-data-relationships-with-enumerations-and-model-classes
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/relationship(_:deleterule:minimummodelcount:maximummodelcount:originalname:inverse:hashmodifier:)
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/preserving-your-apps-model-data-across-launches
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/syncing-model-data-across-a-persons-devices

Scope

Applies to:

- one-to-one, one-to-many, and many-to-many model relationships
- delete-rule selection (`.cascade`, `.nullify`, `.deny`, `.noAction`)
- inverse relationship declaration and optionality constraints
- migration-sensitive relationship evolution in CloudKit-backed stores

Intent

Encode data lifecycle ownership directly in relationship definitions to avoid
orphaned data, invalid references, and sync/migration instability.

Core Principle

Model relationships explicitly and choose delete rules based on data lifecycle intent.

Implementation Rules

- Use `@Relationship` on model references/collections when behavior must be explicit.
- Define inverse relationships explicitly when automatic inference is ambiguous.
- Use `.cascade` when child lifetime is owned by parent.
- Use `.nullify` only when orphaning is valid and expected.
- Prefer optional relationships for CloudKit-compatible schemas.
- Ensure relationship cardinality and optionality align with real business constraints.
- Treat delete-rule changes as migration-sensitive schema evolution.

Canonical Patterns

```swift
@Model
final class Category {
    @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade, inverse: \Item.category)
    var items: [Item] = []
}

@Model
final class Item {
    @Relationship(inverse: \Category.items)
    var category: Category?
}
```

Anti-Patterns

- Relying on implicit delete semantics for critical parent-child data.
- Defining non-optional relationships that conflict with CloudKit sync constraints.
- Omitting inverse where write-order ambiguity can occur.
- Using relationships for static vocabularies better represented by enums.

Checklist

- Is delete behavior intentional for each relationship?
- Is inverse configured or safely inferred?
- Are optionality/cardinality choices CloudKit-compatible where needed?
- Would deletions produce expected graph behavior without orphan leaks?

Architectural Implications

- Relationship modeling defines aggregate boundaries and lifecycle ownership.
- Delete rules should match business invariants and retention obligations.
- Inverse and optionality design must support both local correctness and sync compatibility.

Advanced Notes

- `.cascade` encodes ownership; `.nullify` encodes optional association retention.
- Relationship constraints interact with migration and sync behavior; changes must be versioned carefully.
- Enum-based relationships remain preferable for static taxonomies.

Integration With Architecture

- Validate relationship behavior against service, actor, and direct `ModelContext` delete/update use-cases in `Packages/Data`.
- Ensure inspector/detail UI assumptions align with nullify/cascade outcomes.
- Keep relationship decisions synchronized with schema and migration rules.

Enforcement Summary

Always prefer:

- explicit relationship intent
- delete rules aligned to lifecycle ownership
- explicit inverse where inference is ambiguous
- CloudKit-safe optionality decisions

Never allow:

- implicit critical delete semantics
- relationship constraints that conflict with synchronization model
- business-static vocabularies modeled as dynamic relationships
- orphan/leak risk from unreviewed nullify/cascade choices
